s-v-american-fed-of-state-cty-muni-employees","publisher":"Massachusetts Department of Labor Relations (mass.gov)","title":"DLR Q&A re: Janus v. AFSCME","document_date":null},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:09:24Z","content_sha256":"be82dac0fc704fa097700b92f1fe4f77ac9a8dc2af1ef8470b0d8dc14376d3c8","capture":{"method":"firecrawl"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}}]}},{"id":"3b710d32-7cb7-5d2a-a993-ff3b9b6a5137","payload":{"kind":"mini_lesson","locale":"en","locale_state":"reviewed","title":"Which labor law covers my job?","body":"It depends on who your employer is. In Massachusetts, M.G.L. c. 150E covers state, county and municipal employees in the executive and judicial branches, and employees of certain authorities. Managerial and confidential employees are excluded, and so are some others, such as elected and appointed officials. Most private-sector workers are covered instead by the federal National Labor Relations Act (NLRA). The NLRA's definition of \"employer\" leaves out states and their political subdivisions, so it does not cover state or local government jobs. If you are not sure which law applies to you, ask your steward.","attribution":"","source":"Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School (law.cornell.edu), 29 U.S. Code 152 - Definitions (NLRA), retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/152); National Labor Relations Board (nlrb.gov), Employee Rights, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.nlrb.gov/about-nlrb/rights-we-protect/your-rights/employee-rights); Massachusetts General Court (malegislature.gov), M.G.L. c. 150E, section 1, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleXXI/Chapter150E/