exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}}]}},{"id":"767c887b-965d-547b-a945-d4f75ad54224","payload":{"kind":"mini_lesson","locale":"en","locale_state":"reviewed","title":"Where do my dues go?","body":"Your union's financial reports show how it uses its money. In Massachusetts public employment, c. 150E, s. 14 requires every employee organization to keep adequate financial records and, each year, give members a detailed written financial report: a balance sheet and an operating statement showing all receipts, their sources, and disbursements. A copy is filed with the state labor agency, now the Department of Labor Relations (DLR). Unions covered by federal law file annual reports with the U.S. Department of Labor listing assets, liabilities, payments to officers and employees, and other disbursements with their purposes. The app's \"Where your dues go\" view summarizes those federal filings when they exist.","attribution":"","source":"Massachusetts General Court (malegislature.gov), M.G.L. c. 150E, section 14, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleXXI/Chapter150E/Section14); Massachusetts Office of the State Auditor (mass.gov), Audit of the Department of Labor Relations: overview of the Department of Labor Relations, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.mass.gov/info-details/overview-of-the-department-of-labor-relations); Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School (law.cornell.edu), 29 U.S. Code 431 - Report of labor organizations, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/431)","rights_note":"owner-authored: Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only.","review_note":"source review, 2