equest; no special words are required. The representative is there to help clarify the facts and bring out facts in your favor. The National Labor Relations Board (NLRB) describes a similar right, called Weingarten rights, which under current Board law covers union-represented private-sector workers. If you are called to such a meeting, ask for your steward before answering questions.","attribution":"","source":"National Labor Relations Board (nlrb.gov), Weingarten Rights, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.nlrb.gov/about-nlrb/rights-we-protect/your-rights/weingarten-rights); Massachusetts Department of Labor Relations (mass.gov), F. Prohibited Practices, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.mass.gov/info-details/f-prohibited-practices)","rights_note":"owner-authored: Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only.","review_note":"source review, 2026-10-12T07:41:37Z: Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication.","bullets":[],"direction":"","topics":["faq","rights-at-work"],"audience":"all","tone":"","learning_outcome":"","factual_claims":true,"priority":0,"is_default":false},"provenance":{"content_path":"content/explainer-content/faq.json","unit_id":"faq-can-i-bring-a-steward-to-a-meeting","locale":"en","record":{"provenance_id":"prov-ec820a166494ecf9c49e0c7fde430f795bf46f48c6eb179df4ad016a3ed8df4c","source":{"url":"https://app.example.invalid/#faq-can-i-bring-a-steward-to-a-meeting","publisher":"Union Ground platform content (authored draft)","title":"Can I bring a steward to a meeting with my supervisor?","document_date":"2026-10-12"},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:42:08Z","content_sha256":"0af19fb8fb38fd4614070fba2db25cc82dc83521f60f1d4fed9dfc982fa30e80","capture":{"method":"authored"},"extraction":{"method":"model","extractor":"bilingual draft generation, en+es; quotes checked by quotation validation","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":["prov-c0c297b8befc2b93310f26c155213d827e18c3f0f1247935f02f3d1aed299f0d","prov-ecae6940b308e391c248946ec43aa6ac002944d053d015fb9b902296489b0551"],"review":{"status":"reviewed","reviewed_at":"2026-10-12T07:41:37Z","reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":"Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication."},"reuse":{"basis":"owner-authored","note":"Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only."}},"source_records":[{"source_key":"nlrb-weingarten","provenance":{"provenance_id":"prov-c0c297b8befc2b93310f26c155213d827e18c3f0f1247935f02f3d1aed299f0d","source":{"url":"https://www.nlrb.gov/about-nlrb/rights-we-protect/your-rights/weingarten-rights","publisher":"National Labor Relations Board (nlrb.gov)","title":"Weingarten Rights","document_date":null},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:07:02Z","content_sha256":"b120dedfff40c8299f73d11ad7c870683992e138e5bf6e1f95acc8b49fb68a70","capture":{"method":"http-get"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}},{"source_key":"dlr-f-prohibited","provenance":{"provenance_id":"prov-ecae6940b308e391c248946ec43aa6ac002944d053d015fb9b902296489b0551","source":{"url":"https://www.mass.gov/info-details/f-prohibited-practices","publisher":"Massachusetts Department of Labor Relations (mass.gov)","title":"F. Prohibited Practices","document_date":null},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:09:28Z","content_sha256":"f224cd36a7aed5e7970224b979c7bce2283f952dc3d88f3906e6f8873c11dfd5","capture":{"method":"firecrawl"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}}]}},{"id":"402e3f3e-3dcd-532a-a157-3371448bdc3f","payload":{"kind":"mini_lesson","locale":"es","locale_state":"reviewed","title":"¿Puedo llevar a mi delegado o delegada sindical a una reunión con mi supervisor?","body":"A menudo, sí. En el empleo público de Massachusetts, el Departamento de Relaciones Laborales (DLR) dice que un empleador que le niega representación sindical en una entrevista de investigación que usted cree razonablemente que puede terminar en disciplina interfiere con sus derechos según la ley c. 150E. El derecho nace cuando usted hace una solicitud válida; no se requieren palabras especiales. El representante está ahí para ayudar a aclarar los hechos y destacar los hechos a su favor. La Junta Nacional de Relaciones Laborales (NLRB) describe un derecho parecido, llamado derechos Weingarten (Weingarten rights), que según las reglas actuales de la Junta cubre a los trabajadores del sector privado representados por un sindicato. Si lo llaman a una reunión así, pida a su delegado o delegada sindical antes de responder preguntas.","attribution":"","source":"National Labor Relations Board (nlrb.gov), Weingarten Rights, consultada el 12 oct 2026 (https://www.nlrb.gov/about-nlrb/rights-we-protect/your-rights/weingarten-rights); Massachusetts Department of Labor Relations (mass.gov), F. Prohibited Practices, consultada el 12 oct 2026 (https://www.mass.gov/info-details/f-prohibited-practices)","rights_note":"owner-authored: Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only.","review_note":"source review, 2026-10-12T07:41:37Z: Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication.","bullets":[],"direction":"","topics":["faq","rights-at-work"],"audience":"all","tone":"","learning_outcome":"","factual_claims":true,"priority":0,"is_default":false},"provenance":{"content_path":"content/explainer-content/faq.json","unit_id":"faq-can-i-bring-a-steward-to-a-meeting","locale":"es","record":{"provenance_id":"prov-ec820a166494ecf9c49e0c7fde430f795bf46f48c6eb179df4ad016a3ed8df4c","source":{"url":"https://app.example.invalid/#faq-can-i-bring-a-steward-to-a-meeting","publisher":"Union Ground platform content (authored draft)","title":"Can I bring a steward to a meeting with my supervisor?","document_date":"2026-10-12"},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:42:08Z","content_sha256":"0af19fb8fb38fd4614070fba2db25cc82dc83521f60f1d4fed9dfc982fa30e80","capture":{"method":"authored"},"extraction":{"method":"model","extractor":"bilingual draft generation, en+es; quotes checked by quotation validation","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":["prov-c0c297b8befc2b93310f26c155213d827e18c3f0f1247935f02f3d1aed299f0d","prov-ecae6940b308e391c248946ec43aa6ac002944d053d015fb9b902296489b0551"],"review":{"status":"reviewed","reviewed_at":"2026-10-12T07:41:37Z","reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":"Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication."},"reuse":{"basis":"owner-authored","note":"Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only."}},"source_records":[{"source_key":"nlrb-weingarten","provenance":{"provenance_id":"prov-c0c297b8befc2b93310f26c155213d827e18c3f0f1247935f02f3d1aed299f0d","source":{"url":"https://www.nlrb.gov/about-nlrb/rights-we-protect/your-rights/weingarten-rights","publisher":"National Labor Relations Board (nlrb.gov)","title":"Weingarten Rights","document_date":null},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:07:02Z","content_sha256":"b120dedfff40c8299f73d11ad7c870683992e138e5bf6e1f95acc8b49fb68a70","capture":{"method":"http-get"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}},{"source_key":"dlr-f-prohibited","provenance":{"provenance_id":"prov-ecae6940b308e391c248946ec43aa6ac002944d053d015fb9b902296489b0551","source":{"url":"https://www.mass.gov/info-details/f-prohibited-practices","publisher":"Massachusetts Department of Labor Relations (mass.gov)","title":"F. Prohibited Practices","document_date":null},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:09:28Z","content_sha256":"f224cd36a7aed5e7970224b979c7bce2283f952dc3d88f3906e6f8873c11dfd5","capture":{"method":"firecrawl"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}}]}},{"id":"9e7f1d43-47a8-5820-a0a2-3f8d051e358c","payload":{"kind":"mini_lesson","locale":"en","locale_state":"reviewed","title":"Do I have a right to a union representative at every meeting with management?","body":"No. The right covers investigatory interviews. The Massachusetts Department of Labor Relations (DLR) says a meeting is investigatory when the employer's purpose is to investigate your conduct and get information from you, or to support a later decision to discipline you. If the only purpose is to tell you about, or give you, discipline that was already decided, and there is no investigation, there is no right to union representation. The test is whether a reasonable person in your place would have believed that adverse action would follow. If you are not sure what kind of meeting it is, ask, and contact your steward.","attribution":"","source":"Massachusetts Department of Labor Relations (mass.gov), F. Prohibited Practices, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.mass.gov/info-details/f-prohibited-practices)","rights_note":"owner-authored: Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only.","review_note":"source review, 2026-10-12T07:41:37Z: Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication.","bullets":[],"direction":"","topics":["faq","rights-at-work"],"audience":"all","tone":"","learning_outcome":"","factual_claims":true,"priority":0,"is_default":false},"provenance":{"content_path":"content/explainer-content/faq.json","unit_id":"faq-is-every-meeting-covered","locale":"en","record":{"provenance_id":"prov-039382a869e0691ff5371901188a987ad37c0a24637ae6d5c8bc83a994df916a","source":{"url":"https://app.example.invalid/#faq-is-every-meeting-covered","publisher":"Union Ground platform content (authored draft)","title":"Do I have a right to a union representative at every meeting with management?","document_date":"2026-10-12"},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:42:08Z","content_sha256":"5132511a3df36a1b0e98a0f6db42876de1b7448b73f290f1d4bb64012644feab","capture":{"method":"authored"},"extraction":{"method":"model","extractor":"bilingual draft generation, en+es; quotes checked by quotation validation","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":["prov-ecae6940b308e391c248946ec43aa6ac002944d053d015fb9b902296489b0551"],"review":{"status":"reviewed","reviewed_at":"2026-10-12T07:41:37Z","reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":"Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication."},"reuse":{"basis":"owner-authored","note":"Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only."}},"source_records":[{"source_key":"dlr-f-prohibited","provenance":{"provenance_id":"prov-ecae6940b308e391c248946ec43aa6ac002944d053d015fb9b902296489b0551","source":{"url":"https://www.mass.gov/info-details/f-prohibited-practices","publisher":"Massachusetts Department of Labor Relations (mass.gov)","title":"F. Prohibited Practices","document_date":null},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:09:28Z","content_sha256":"f224cd36a7aed5e7970224b979c7bce2283f952dc3d88f3906e6f8873c11dfd5","capture":{"method":"firecrawl"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}}]}},{"id":"25e02f1a-b1be-5b54-ae50-5380c80cccc8","payload":{"kind":"mini_lesson","locale":"es","locale_state":"reviewed","title":"¿Tengo derecho a un representante sindical en cada reunión con la gerencia?","body":"No. El derecho cubre las entrevistas de investigación. El Departamento de Relaciones Laborales de Massachusetts (DLR) dice que una reunión es de investigación cuando el propósito del empleador es investigar la conducta de usted y obtener información de usted, o apoyar una decisión posterior de disciplinarlo. Si el único propósito es informarle o entregarle una medida disciplinaria ya decidida, y no hay investigación, no existe el derecho a representación sindical. La prueba es si una persona razonable en su lugar habría creído que vendría una medida en su contra. Si no está seguro de qué tipo de reunión es, pregunte y comuníquese con su delegado o delegada sindical.","attribution":"","source":"Massachusetts Department of Labor Relations (mass.gov), F. Prohibited Practices, consultada el 12 oct 2026 (https://www.mass.gov/info-details/f-prohibited-practices)","rights_note":"owner-authored: Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only.","review_note":"source review, 2026-10-12T07:41:37Z: Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication.","bullets":[],"direction":"","topics":["faq","rights-at-work"],"audience":"all","tone":"","learning_outcome":"","factual_claims":true,"priority":0,"is_default":false},"provenance":{"content_path":"content/explainer-content/faq.json","unit_id":"faq-is-every-meeting-covered","locale":"es","record":{"provenance_id":"prov-039382a869e0691ff5371901188a987ad37c0a24637ae6d5c8bc83a994df916a","source":{"url":"https://app.example.invalid/#faq-is-every-meeting-covered","publisher":"Union Ground platform content (authored draft)","title":"Do I have a right to a union representative at every meeting with management?","document_date":"2026-10-12"},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:42:08Z","content_sha256":"5132511a3df36a1b0e98a0f6db42876de1b7448b73f290f1d4bb64012644feab","capture":{"method":"authored"},"extraction":{"method":"model","extractor":"bilingual draft generation, en+es; quotes checked by quotation validation","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":["prov-ecae6940b308e391c248946ec43aa6ac002944d053d015fb9b902296489b0551"],"review":{"status":"reviewed","reviewed_at":"2026-10-12T07:41:37Z","reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":"Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication."},"reuse":{"basis":"owner-authored","note":"Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only."}},"source_records":[{"source_key":"dlr-f-prohibited","provenance":{"provenance_id":"prov-ecae6940b308e391c248946ec43aa6ac002944d053d015fb9b902296489b0551","source":{"url":"https://www.mass.gov/info-details/f-prohibited-practices","publisher":"Massachusetts Department of Labor Relations (mass.gov)","title":"F. Prohibited Practices","document_date":null},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:09:28Z","content_sha256":"f224cd36a7aed5e7970224b979c7bce2283f952dc3d88f3906e6f8873c11dfd5","capture":{"method":"firecrawl"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}}]}},{"id":"ccc2b0b1-320c-5811-a602-19b28c165aba","payload":{"kind":"mini_lesson","locale":"en","locale_state":"reviewed","title":"What is a Loudermill hearing?","body":"The name comes from the U.S. Supreme Court case Cleveland Board of Education v. Loudermill (1985). The Court held that a tenured public employee is entitled, before termination, to oral or written notice of the charges, an explanation of the employer's evidence, and a chance to tell their side of the story. The Court said this pretermination hearing, though necessary, need not be elaborate, because procedures after termination also exist. In Massachusetts, the civil service law (c. 31, s. 41) separately requires written notice and a full hearing before a tenured civil service employee is discharged. Ask your steward which protections apply to your job.","attribution":"","source":"Massachusetts General Court (malegislature.gov), M.G.L. c. 31, section 41, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleIV/Chapter31/Section41); Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School (law.cornell.edu), Cleveland Board of Education v. Loudermill, 470 U.S. 532 (1985), retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/470/532)","rights_note":"owner-authored: Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only.","review_note":"source review, 2026-10-12T07:41:37Z: Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication.","bullets":[],"direction":"","topics":["faq","rights-at-work"],"audience":"all","tone":"","learning_outcome":"","factual_claims":true,"priority":0,"is_default":false},"provenance":{"content_path":"content/explainer-content/faq.json","unit_id":"faq-what-is-a-loudermill-hearing","locale":"en","record":{"provenance_id":"prov-d41c15233fa1d78872cd139059974c59f78726a79ba503a189824e4b8c815f24","source":{"url":"https://app.example.invalid/#faq-what-is-a-loudermill-hearing","publisher":"Union Ground platform content (authored draft)","title":"What is a Loudermill hearing?","document_date":"2026-10-12"},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:42:08Z","content_sha256":"a2f84246257141c94cc6f798cbf154295853a8bccc8076705be533f8349a1f37","capture":{"method":"authored"},"extraction":{"method":"model","extractor":"bilingual draft generation, en+es; quotes checked by quotation validation","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":["prov-657cb2ea203fd171135d0e7f70dc54422182ba530b342c1049526a27c8ffd0b2","prov-bc7e94dc38ca13bc6503158531938a970f38867b36013418a26d455cb5ce2f2c"],"review":{"status":"reviewed","reviewed_at":"2026-10-12T07:41:37Z","reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":"Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication."},"reuse":{"basis":"owner-authored","note":"Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only."}},"source_records":[{"source_key":"ma-31-s41","provenance":{"provenance_id":"prov-657cb2ea203fd171135d0e7f70dc54422182ba530b342c1049526a27c8ffd0b2","source":{"url":"https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleIV/Chapter31/Section41","publisher":"Massachusetts General Court (malegislature.gov)","title":"M.G.L. c. 31, section 41","document_date":null},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:04:58Z","content_sha256":"be7da4f1f7a2936b9af4c28f22b61c7bf89fe5f31669464952e2e127f103a3a3","capture":{"method":"http-get"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}},{"source_key":"lii-loudermill","provenance":{"provenance_id":"prov-bc7e94dc38ca13bc6503158531938a970f38867b36013418a26d455cb5ce2f2c","source":{"url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/470/532","publisher":"Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School (law.cornell.edu)","title":"Cleveland Board of Education v. Loudermill, 470 U.S. 532 (1985)","document_date":"1985-03-19"},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:07:16Z","content_sha256":"34a87658b56672fea23ed52e6bba8c284954d488c094292922d3b9f9b1f1d8e2","capture":{"method":"http-get"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"1985-03-19","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}}]}},{"id":"13cd572b-c8bc-511b-ad68-9ad8db905587","payload":{"kind":"mini_lesson","locale":"es","locale_state":"reviewed","title":"¿Qué es una audiencia Loudermill?","body":"El nombre viene del caso de la Corte Suprema de EE. UU. Cleveland Board of Education v. Loudermill (1985). La Corte decidió que un empleado público con permanencia (tenured) tiene derecho, antes del despido, a un aviso oral o escrito de los cargos, una explicación de las pruebas del empleador y la oportunidad de contar su versión de los hechos. La Corte dijo que esta audiencia previa al despido es necesaria, pero no tiene que ser complicada, porque también existen procedimientos después del despido. En Massachusetts, la ley de servicio civil (c. 31, sección 41) exige por separado un aviso por escrito y una audiencia completa antes de despedir a un empleado de servicio civil con permanencia. Pregunte a su delegado o delegada sindical qué protecciones aplican a su empleo.","attribution":"","source":"Massachusetts General Court (malegislature.gov), M.G.L. c. 31, section 41, consultada el 12 oct 2026 (https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleIV/Chapter31/Section41); Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School (law.cornell.edu), Cleveland Board of Education v. Loudermill, 470 U.S. 532 (1985), consultada el 12 oct 2026 (https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/470/532)","rights_note":"owner-authored: Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only.","review_note":"source review, 2026-10-12T07:41:37Z: Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication.","bullets":[],"direction":"","topics":["faq","rights-at-work"],"audience":"all","tone":"","learning_outcome":"","factual_claims":true,"priority":0,"is_default":false},"provenance":{"content_path":"content/explainer-content/faq.json","unit_id":"faq-what-is-a-loudermill-hearing","locale":"es","record":{"provenance_id":"prov-d41c15233fa1d78872cd139059974c59f78726a79ba503a189824e4b8c815f24","source":{"url":"https://app.example.invalid/#faq-what-is-a-loudermill-hearing","publisher":"Union Ground platform content (authored draft)","title":"What is a Loudermill hearing?","document_date":"2026-10-12"},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:42:08Z","content_sha256":"a2f84246257141c94cc6f798cbf154295853a8bccc8076705be533f8349a1f37","capture":{"method":"authored"},"extraction":{"method":"model","extractor":"bilingual draft generation, en+es; quotes checked by quotation validation","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":["prov-657cb2ea203fd171135d0e7f70dc54422182ba530b342c1049526a27c8ffd0b2","prov-bc7e94dc38ca13bc6503158531938a970f38867b36013418a26d455cb5ce2f2c"],"review":{"status":"reviewed","reviewed_at":"2026-10-12T07:41:37Z","reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":"Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication."},"reuse":{"basis":"owner-authored","note":"Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only."}},"source_records":[{"source_key":"ma-31-s41","provenance":{"provenance_id":"prov-657cb2ea203fd171135d0e7f70dc54422182ba530b342c1049526a27c8ffd0b2","source":{"url":"https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleIV/Chapter31/Section41","publisher":"Massachusetts General Court (malegislature.gov)","title":"M.G.L. c. 31, section 41","document_date":null},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:04:58Z","content_sha256":"be7da4f1f7a2936b9af4c28f22b61c7bf89fe5f31669464952e2e127f103a3a3","capture":{"method":"http-get"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}},{"source_key":"lii-loudermill","provenance":{"provenance_id":"prov-bc7e94dc38ca13bc6503158531938a970f38867b36013418a26d455cb5ce2f2c","source":{"url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/470/532","publisher":"Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School (law.cornell.edu)","title":"Cleveland Board of Education v. Loudermill, 470 U.S. 532 (1985)","document_date":"1985-03-19"},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:07:16Z","content_sha256":"34a87658b56672fea23ed52e6bba8c284954d488c094292922d3b9f9b1f1d8e2","capture":{"method":"http-get"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"1985-03-19","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}}]}},{"id":"0f579b59-3a7e-57df-a488-5d3bdb83d550","payload":{"kind":"mini_lesson","locale":"en","locale_state":"reviewed","title":"What are Garrity rights?","body":"The name comes from Garrity v. New Jersey (1967). In that case, police officers were questioned and given a choice: answer, or lose their jobs. The U.S. Supreme Court said the choice was either to forfeit their jobs or to incriminate themselves. It held that statements obtained under threat of removal from office cannot be used in later criminal proceedings, and that this protection extends to everyone, police officers or anyone else. Whether Garrity applies to a particular interview is a legal question that depends on the facts. Talk with your steward, and with a lawyer if criminal charges are possible.","attribution":"","source":"Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School (law.cornell.edu), Garrity v. New Jersey, 385 U.S. 493 (1967), retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/385/493)","rights_note":"owner-authored: Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only.","review_note":"source review, 2026-10-12T07:41:37Z: Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication.","bullets":[],"direction":"","topics":["faq","rights-at-work"],"audience":"all","tone":"","learning_outcome":"","factual_claims":true,"priority":0,"is_default":false},"provenance":{"content_path":"content/explainer-content/faq.json","unit_id":"faq-what-are-garrity-rights","locale":"en","record":{"provenance_id":"prov-f4ee9af1a347069268a6a55e7506c163d5edd68b64bceb3171200bcd1d62e983","source":{"url":"https://app.example.invalid/#faq-what-are-garrity-rights","publisher":"Union Ground platform content (authored draft)","title":"What are Garrity rights?","document_date":"2026-10-12"},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:42:08Z","content_sha256":"1e2981c1ae0f3735b2cbc6bbd3a5bd85974e5c9fdd775e08e77939bb25c434d4","capture":{"method":"authored"},"extraction":{"method":"model","extractor":"bilingual draft generation, en+es; quotes checked by quotation validation","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":["prov-b611192fb8764a9880f2609e565cd2992001d1dd8059ec1e991f9237b1302b8b"],"review":{"status":"reviewed","reviewed_at":"2026-10-12T07:41:37Z","reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":"Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication."},"reuse":{"basis":"owner-authored","note":"Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only."}},"source_records":[{"source_key":"lii-garrity","provenance":{"provenance_id":"prov-b611192fb8764a9880f2609e565cd2992001d1dd8059ec1e991f9237b1302b8b","source":{"url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/385/493","publisher":"Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School (law.cornell.edu)","title":"Garrity v. New Jersey, 385 U.S. 493 (1967)","document_date":"1967-01-16"},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:07:17Z","content_sha256":"a64f277b9b485df71dc7b5f9cb6486fea30ccc1f338a98e3726feb0472d337fe","capture":{"method":"http-get"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"1967-01-16","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}}]}},{"id":"8e2f798c-f424-5b84-a3d6-3b2d7f076e91","payload":{"kind":"mini_lesson","locale":"es","locale_state":"reviewed","title":"¿Qué son los derechos Garrity?","body":"El nombre viene del caso Garrity v. New Jersey (1967). En ese caso, a unos policías los interrogaron y les dieron a elegir: responder o perder el empleo. La Corte Suprema de EE. UU. dijo que la opción era perder el empleo o incriminarse. Decidió que las declaraciones obtenidas bajo amenaza de destitución no se pueden usar después en un proceso penal, y que esta protección se extiende a todos, sean policías o cualquier otra persona. Si Garrity se aplica a una entrevista en particular es una pregunta legal que depende de los hechos. Hable con su delegado o delegada sindical, y con un abogado si es posible que haya cargos penales.","attribution":"","source":"Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School (law.cornell.edu), Garrity v. New Jersey, 385 U.S. 493 (1967), consultada el 12 oct 2026 (https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/385/493)","rights_note":"owner-authored: Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only.","review_note":"source review, 2026-10-12T07:41:37Z: Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication.","bullets":[],"direction":"","topics":["faq","rights-at-work"],"audience":"all","tone":"","learning_outcome":"","factual_claims":true,"priority":0,"is_default":false},"provenance":{"content_path":"content/explainer-content/faq.json","unit_id":"faq-what-are-garrity-rights","locale":"es","record":{"provenance_id":"prov-f4ee9af1a347069268a6a55e7506c163d5edd68b64bceb3171200bcd1d62e983","source":{"url":"https://app.example.invalid/#faq-what-are-garrity-rights","publisher":"Union Ground platform content (authored draft)","title":"What are Garrity rights?","document_date":"2026-10-12"},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:42:08Z","content_sha256":"1e2981c1ae0f3735b2cbc6bbd3a5bd85974e5c9fdd775e08e77939bb25c434d4","capture":{"method":"authored"},"extraction":{"method":"model","extractor":"bilingual draft generation, en+es; quotes checked by quotation validation","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":["prov-b611192fb8764a9880f2609e565cd2992001d1dd8059ec1e991f9237b1302b8b"],"review":{"status":"reviewed","reviewed_at":"2026-10-12T07:41:37Z","reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":"Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication."},"reuse":{"basis":"owner-authored","note":"Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only."}},"source_records":[{"source_key":"lii-garrity","provenance":{"provenance_id":"prov-b611192fb8764a9880f2609e565cd2992001d1dd8059ec1e991f9237b1302b8b","source":{"url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/385/493","publisher":"Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School (law.cornell.edu)","title":"Garrity v. New Jersey, 385 U.S. 493 (1967)","document_date":"1967-01-16"},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:07:17Z","content_sha256":"a64f277b9b485df71dc7b5f9cb6486fea30ccc1f338a98e3726feb0472d337fe","capture":{"method":"http-get"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"1967-01-16","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}}]}},{"id":"6b0d51e1-7b51-5b09-af1c-99ec4b7903a1","payload":{"kind":"mini_lesson","locale":"en","locale_state":"reviewed","title":"Can I talk about my pay with my coworkers?","body":"For most private-sector workers, yes. The National Labor Relations Board (NLRB) says employees covered by the National Labor Relations Act (NLRA) have the right to talk with coworkers about wages, and policies that ban those talks are unlawful. The NLRA does not cover state or local government employers. In Massachusetts public employment, c. 150E protects lawful concerted activities for mutual aid or protection, and the Department of Labor Relations (DLR) lists joining together to investigate wages as protected activity. The NLRB also notes that rules about using your employer's equipment may still apply. If you are told not to discuss pay, write down what was said and talk with your steward.","attribution":"","source":"Massachusetts Department of Labor Relations (mass.gov), C. Employee Rights to Organize and Bargain Collectively, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.mass.gov/info-details/c-employee-rights-to-organize-and-bargain-collectively); Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School (law.cornell.edu), 29 U.S. Code 152 - Definitions (NLRA), retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/152); National Labor Relations Board (nlrb.gov), Your Rights to Discuss Wages, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.nlrb.gov/about-nlrb/rights-we-protect/your-rights/your-rights-to-discuss-wages); Massachusetts General Court (malegislature.gov), M.G.L. c. 150E, section 2, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleXXI/Chapter150E/Section2)","rights_note":"owner-authored: Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only.","review_note":"source review, 2026-10-12T07:41:37Z: Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication.","bullets":[],"direction":"","topics":["faq","rights-at-work"],"audience":"all","tone":"","learning_outcome":"","factual_claims":true,"priority":0,"is_default":false},"provenance":{"content_path":"content/explainer-content/faq.json","unit_id":"faq-can-i-talk-about-my-pay","locale":"en","record":{"provenance_id":"prov-2a9ce7c0d147ff15284e345fbc361d9d360363570105b01b5d2ea9673dc92f26","source":{"url":"https://app.example.invalid/#faq-can-i-talk-about-my-pay","publisher":"Union Ground platform content (authored draft)","title":"Can I talk about my pay with my coworkers?","document_date":"2026-10-12"},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:42:08Z","content_sha256":"5955d55be1acedeb0ce2281e9cb18bdc85795241a910ccab1bd115acaa6c57ca","capture":{"method":"authored"},"extraction":{"method":"model","extractor":"bilingual draft generation, en+es; quotes checked by quotation validation","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":["prov-6473f4f511b5363ca750d7ad44e1eb333241e658a9f60467349afd54d6e12501","prov-12171ec99baffa229634d78d17382025affed91de92a3f43a6893ea0faa9e051","prov-b4f97dd512f7aa26017b563ab5584d53eb4fe725b5879fd48ab037e0eb68a5f6","prov-126932b67a9f47b18d5e6fbf72e8e8f9d02d5bd1e8c15f9323576ec