rovenance":{"provenance_id":"prov-b4f97dd512f7aa26017b563ab5584d53eb4fe725b5879fd48ab037e0eb68a5f6","source":{"url":"https://www.nlrb.gov/about-nlrb/rights-we-protect/your-rights/your-rights-to-discuss-wages","publisher":"National Labor Relations Board (nlrb.gov)","title":"Your Rights to Discuss Wages","document_date":null},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:07:06Z","content_sha256":"50efe865af8a64a1058b1175720ddfe3fd9bf684c0d17f16453ae75f1bd35fd0","capture":{"method":"http-get"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}},{"source_key":"ma-150e-s2","provenance":{"provenance_id":"prov-126932b67a9f47b18d5e6fbf72e8e8f9d02d5bd1e8c15f9323576ecb22e2fbce","source":{"url":"https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleXXI/Chapter150E/Section2","publisher":"Massachusetts General Court (malegislature.gov)","title":"M.G.L. c. 150E, section 2","document_date":null},"retrieved_at":"2026-10-12T07:04:32Z","content_sha256":"0ebbd12fb8b21954f920a48d5eb9c06c655996c8305010873f260f821aac1207","capture":{"method":"http-get"},"extraction":{"method":"verbatim","extractor":"public source capture text view","quote_binding":"exact-substring"},"as_of":"2026-10-12","derived_from":[],"review":{"status":"unreviewed","reviewed_at":null,"reviewer_role":"content reviewer","note":""},"reuse":{"basis":"public-record","note":"Cited and paraphrased; not republished in full."}}}]}},{"id":"4af73e22-6d64-5d8b-a140-60d8664dd183","payload":{"kind":"mini_lesson","locale":"en","locale_state":"reviewed","title":"Can my employer punish me for union activity?","body":"Not lawfully. In Massachusetts public employment, c. 150E, s. 10 makes it a prohibited practice for an employer to discriminate in hiring, tenure or any term of employment to encourage or discourage union membership. It is also prohibited to discharge or discriminate against you for filing a complaint, giving testimony under the law, or joining or choosing to be represented by a union. If the Department of Labor Relations (DLR) finds that an employee was fired or discriminated against in violation of the law, it must order reinstatement, with or without back pay. Most private-sector workers have similar protection under the National Labor Relations Act. A charge at the DLR must be filed within six months, so talk with your steward quickly.","attribution":"","source":"Massachusetts Department of Labor Relations (mass.gov), Public sector unfair labor practice charge, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.mass.gov/how-to/public-sector-unfair-labor-practice-charge); Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School (law.cornell.edu), 29 U.S. Code 158 - Unfair labor practices, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/158); Massachusetts General Court (malegislature.gov), M.G.L. c. 150E, section 10, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleXXI/Chapter150E/Section10); Massachusetts General Court (malegislature.gov), M.G.L. c. 150E, section 11, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleXXI/Chapter150E/Section11)","rights_note":"owner-authored: Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only.","review_note":"source review, 2026-10-12T07:41:37Z: Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication.","bullets":[],"direction":"","topics":["faq","rights-at-work"],"audience":"all","tone":"","learning_outcome":"","f