son","locale":"en","locale_state":"reviewed","title":"Who can file a grievance? Can I present one myself?","body":"Grievances can be filed by one employee or a group. In Massachusetts public employment, c. 150E, s. 5 says you may present a grievance to your employer and have it heard without the union stepping in, as long as the union has the chance to be present at those meetings and any settlement is consistent with the contract in effect. Taking a grievance to arbitration at the Department of Labor Relations (DLR) is different: the DLR says an employer, an employee organization, or both may petition to start grievance arbitration. Your contract's procedure also matters, so talk with your steward before you decide.","attribution":"","source":"Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School (law.cornell.edu), Wex: grievance, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/grievance); Massachusetts General Court (malegislature.gov), M.G.L. c. 150E, section 5, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://malegislature.gov/Laws/GeneralLaws/PartI/TitleXXI/Chapter150E/Section5); Massachusetts Department of Labor Relations (mass.gov), File a petition for arbitration, retrieved Oct 12, 2026 (https://www.mass.gov/how-to/file-a-petition-for-arbitration)","rights_note":"owner-authored: Original plain-language text drafted for the platform; statutes, regulations, agency pages and court opinions are cited and paraphrased, short quotations only.","review_note":"source review, 2026-10-12T07:41:37Z: Fresh-context verification pass: every claim read against its verbatim source quotes in context, Spanish checked against English, jurisdiction and affiliation guardrails checked. Not yet reviewed by the platform editor; editor review is required before publication.","bullets":[],"direction":"","topics":["faq","grievances"],"audience":"all","tone":"","learning_outcome":"","factual_claims":true,"priority":0,"is_default":false},"provenance":{"content_path":"content/explainer-content/faq.json"